help.body:
## The three tabs

- **Re-play** shows recorded runs. Pick one in the library on the left, or press the button to load the run you opened last.
- **Create** is where you build your own run: write a task, choose a pattern, add and connect agents, then press Run.
- **Result** shows the answer and the numbers of a run. It is light green when there is a result to see.

## Using the replay

- **Play and pause** start and stop the replay. **Speed** slows it down or speeds it up.
- **Previous step and Next step** move one message at a time. **The bar** lets you jump to any point.
- **The agent cards** show who is working, waiting or done. Click a card to read that agent's steps, messages and output.
- **Click a step** to read its exact message.
- In a debate, a badge shows which round it is in.

### Replay speed {#replay-speed}

How fast the recorded run plays. A run is replayed from its record, so a slow one can be watched quickly and nothing is sent to a model. Speeds are 1x, 2x and so on. With the side layout one button cycles through them; the layout is chosen in Settings, Player controls.

{related: settings-player, replay-steps}

### Steps and tools {#replay-steps}

Everything the chosen agent did, one line for each step: the model call, a web search, a retry. Select a step to see its details. The player decides how far the run is revealed.

{related: replay-messages, replay-output}

### Messages {#replay-messages}

What passed between the agents: who sent what to whom. In a debate or with a supervisor you see each instruction and each answer in order.

{related: replay-steps, harness-handoff}

### Agent output {#replay-output}

The full answer of the chosen agent. A handoff cut or a reply that reached its token limit is marked here, and the whole answer is kept.

{related: replay-steps, harness-output, harness-handoff}

### Warnings in a replay {#replay-warnings}

A warning means the run went on but something needed your attention: a busy provider and a new try, an answer that was cut, or a limit that stopped the run. It names the agent and, when the run recorded it, the time. Open the related section for what the setting does and how to change it.

{related: harness-retries, harness-steps, harness-cost}

### How it ended {#result-outcome}

Whether the run finished, failed, was interrupted or was stopped by a limit, and which limit. A run stopped by a limit keeps everything done so far, and Retry carries on from the agents that finished.

{related: result-limits, harness-steps}

### Estimated cost {#result-cost}

What the run cost by the list prices in the catalog. An agent on a free Gemini key counts as free. The real bill at the provider can differ a little, so treat it as a close estimate.

{related: harness-cost, create-estimate}

### Tokens per agent {#result-agents}

What each agent used: its time, the tokens it read (input) and wrote (output), and its share of the time. Tokens are what you pay for, and the agents that write the most usually cost the most.

{related: harness-tokens, result-cost}

### Limits {#result-limits}

What the run and each agent used of their limits. A limit marked run is the limit of the whole run; an agent can have a lower one of its own, and the lower one wins. A limit that was reached is marked.

{related: harness-steps, harness-tokens, harness-cost}

### Untrusted web content {#replay-web-content}

Search results are text that came from the web, so they are marked as data, not instructions. The agent was told so, but a model can still be fooled by a page written to look like instructions. If an agent did something odd after a search, read the results here first.

{related: harness-web-content, harness-web-search}

## The library and the run menu

- The library lists the example runs and your own. Use the search box to filter it.
- The cog on a run opens the run menu: **About** (title, author, link and a description), **Export run** (a JSON file), **Use in Create** (opens the run's task and design on the Create tab), **Export definition** (only the task and the design) and, for your own runs, **Delete**.
- On your own runs a round icon next to the cog shows how the run ended (green check: finished, amber square: stopped, red cross: failed), and colored chips name the providers the run used.
- **Submit to library** (your finished runs) saves the run as a JSON file and opens an e-mail to the Agent Atlas team. Attach the file and send it; nothing is sent from the site itself. The run holds the whole task and every answer, so check that it has no private information. Runs that are added are published under CC BY 4.0.
- The arrow button imports a run from a JSON file. Export run leaves out your language, time zone and browser.

## Creating a run

- Choose a pattern: sequential, parallel, supervisor or debate. Each one starts from the agents it needs.
- Choose the provider with the tiles above the diagram: Anthropic, Gemini free (free models only) and Gemini paid (all models). You can pick more than one. A dot on a tile shows its key: green when it is loaded, yellow when it is saved but locked, none when there is no key. A tile without a key opens its key section in Settings.
- The **Default model** list sets the model of every agent. Select an agent to give it a model of its own, or set it back to Default. Agents may use different providers in one run; each agent uses the key of its provider.
- Add agents from the palette and connect them with the arrow on each box. Drag the boxes to arrange them.
- Select an agent to change its model, web search, name and instructions. A free agent is yours to define. Open **Limits of this agent** to give it its own limits (steps, tokens, tokens of one reply, web searches) and to choose what happens when it reaches one: stop the whole run, or pass on what it wrote and go on. An empty box means the limits of the run apply; the lower one always wins.
- Under the diagram you see an estimate and the limits for the run. A yellow warning appears when the design will probably reach a limit. A run stops as soon as it reaches a limit, and what happened so far is saved.
- **Load definition** and **Clear definition** replace the task and the diagram.

### Task {#create-task}

What you want the agents to do, in at most 1,000 characters. Every agent sees it. A clear task with a clear end gives the best result, and a short one costs less.

{related: create-pattern, create-estimate}

### Orchestration pattern {#create-pattern}

The pattern decides how the agents work together: who works when and who sees what. Sequential passes a result from one agent to the next, parallel lets several work at once and one combines the results, a supervisor hands out work, and in a debate agents argue in rounds and a judge decides. It also decides which connections the diagram allows, so changing it clears the diagram.

{related: create-diagram, harness-steps}

### Diagram {#create-diagram}

Add agents from the palette and connect them with the arrow on each box; drag the boxes to arrange them. Select an agent to change its model, web search, name and instructions. At most 8 agents.

{related: create-pattern, create-limits}

### Limits for this run {#create-limits}

The most a run may use: steps, tokens and web searches. A run stops as soon as it reaches one, and what happened so far is saved. The values start from your Harness settings and can be changed for this one run.

{related: harness-steps, harness-tokens, harness-searches}

### Estimated cost {#create-estimate}

The most this run is likely to cost, worked out from the number of agents, your limits and the prices in the catalog. It is a ceiling, not a promise: a run usually costs less, and the real bill at the provider can differ a little. Web searches and a free Gemini key change it.

{related: harness-cost, harness-tokens}

### Check and run {#create-run}

Before you run, check the agents, the limits and the cost. The key of each provider the design uses must be loaded. A yellow warning says the design will probably reach a limit.

{related: create-estimate, create-limits}

### Tools of an agent {#create-tools}

A tool is something an agent can do besides writing text. Today there is one: web search. Give a tool only to the agents that need it, so a mistake or a poisoned web page can reach as little as possible (least privilege). A free agent that only reasons does not need the web.

If web search is turned off in Settings, Harness, Tools, an agent with the switch on will not search, and the editor tells you.

{related: harness-web-search, harness-web-content, create-limits}

## When a run fails

- The agent that failed blinks red in the diagram and has a red **Retry** button, in Create right after the run and when you open the failed run from Play or Result.
- **Retry** carries the run on from that agent: the agents that finished keep their answers and are not paid for again, and the new events are added to the same saved run. Its message tells what this try took and cost, then the cost of the whole run.
- The arrow next to Retry opens a list of models, Anthropic and Gemini, with the one that failed marked, and the one the harness suggests marked suggested (a model of the same provider; see Fallback model in Settings, Harness, Fallback model). Pick one to retry that agent with another model, for example when a model is busy. If you set Fallback model to Switch automatically, an agent that is out of tries gets one more try with that model by itself, and the replay says so.
- If the key is not in this tab, Create opens with the key fields and a **Carry on the failed run** button. Important messages also show in a message in the middle of the screen that stays until you close it.
- Warnings in a replay show the date and time they happened. A request that gets no answer in time ends with a timeout message that names the provider; you can change how long to wait in Settings.

## Settings

Settings has tabs on the left:

- **API keys**: one tab for each provider. Gemini has a free key and a paid key, so both can be saved at once (a Free key / Paid key switch). Each provider has a color; model tags in replays use it, a cheaper model a paler shade and a stronger one a darker shade. Your keys stay in this browser tab by default. You can save them on this computer, encrypted with a passphrase; one passphrase unlocks every saved key.
- **Harness**: the settings of a run, on the pages Limits, Retries, Fallback model, Cost, Guards and Tools (each setting has a ? button that explains it). Fixed harness lists the rules that always apply. See the next topic.
- **Player controls**: the player buttons beside the diagram or in a slim bar under it.
- **General**: turn off the tooltips (the message in the middle of the screen when a run ends) and the ? help buttons.
- **Data**: back up and restore your runs and settings, or delete them.
- **Shortcuts**: turn keyboard shortcuts on or off and change the go-to letters.
- **Security**: unlock, change or forget the passphrase of a saved key. A red exclamation mark shows when a saved key needs unlocking.

### API keys {#settings-keys}

Your API keys let the site call the models for you. Each provider has its own tab, and Gemini has a free and a paid key, so both can be saved. By default a key stays in this browser tab and is gone when you close it. You can save it on this computer, encrypted with a passphrase; one passphrase unlocks every saved key.

Keys are used only for the calls to the providers. The site never stores them anywhere else.

{related: settings-security, harness-cost}

### General {#settings-general}

Options that change how the site itself behaves, not how a run works: the messages in the middle of the screen and the ? help buttons.

{related: settings-tooltips, settings-help-buttons}

### Tooltips {#settings-tooltips}

Short messages, such as how a run ended, appear in the middle of the screen for a few seconds. Click anywhere or press Escape to close one. You can turn them off here.

{related: settings-help-buttons}

### Help buttons {#settings-help-buttons}

The round ? buttons next to settings in Settings, Create and Play open a short explanation taken from Help. Tick the box to hide them everywhere. Help itself stays in the menu.

{related: settings-tooltips}

### Player controls {#settings-player}

Where the player buttons are: in a column to the right of the diagram (side) or on a slim bar under it. The side layout gives small buttons and more room for the diagram.

{related: settings-shortcuts}

### Shortcuts {#settings-shortcuts}

Keyboard shortcuts for the player and for going to a page. You can turn them off or change the go-to letters. Press ? anywhere to see the list.

{related: settings-player}

### Data on this device {#settings-data}

Everything you do is kept in this browser: your runs, your settings and, only if you chose it, your encrypted keys. Back up writes it to a file, Restore reads such a file, and Delete removes your runs. Nothing is sent to a server.

{related: settings-security}

### Security {#settings-security}

Unlock, change or forget the passphrase of a saved key. A red exclamation mark shows when a saved key needs unlocking. Forgetting the passphrase deletes the saved keys; they cannot be recovered.

{related: settings-keys, settings-data}

## The harness {#harness}

The harness is everything around the model: the loop of agents, the limits, the time cap, the retries and the rules for keys. Agent Atlas has one, and Settings, Harness shows it in pages: Limits, Retries, Cost and Guards hold what you can set, with a ? button next to every setting that opens a short explanation, and Fixed harness holds the rules that cannot change.

- **Adjustable harness**: the pages Limits, Retries, Fallback model, Cost, Guards and Tools. Save keeps them in this browser; they apply to new runs and to Retry. The server clips every value to its range, so nobody can go over the ceiling. Every setting has a ? button that opens its section from this topic.
- **Fixed harness**: the rules that cannot be changed: the total time of a run (270 seconds), 8 agents in a design, 1,000 characters for the task, the size of a request, the pauses of the model in one turn, the runs per hour, and that your keys stay in the browser and are never stored on the server.

When a limit stops a run, the replay shows a warning that names the limit and its value, and the message under the player says the run stopped at your limit. What happened until then is saved.

A single agent can have limits of its own too, set in Create (see Limits of this agent). The Statistics tab of a result has a Limits table: what the whole run and each agent used of their limits, with the limit that was reached marked.

### Harness profile {#harness-profile}

A profile sets the five limits of the Limits page in one click: Careful costs the least, Balanced is the default, and Generous lets long runs finish. Move any slider and the profile becomes Custom. Retries, the cost cap and the guards are not part of a profile.

{related: harness-steps, harness-tokens, harness-cost}

### Maximum steps {#harness-steps}

How many times the agents may ask the model in one run, counting every agent and every try. A run stops at this number, and the replay says so. 1 to 60, default 20.

Raise it for long chains or debates. Lower it to keep a run short.

{related: harness-tokens, harness-cost, harness-retries}

### Maximum tokens per run {#harness-tokens}

The input and output of all agents together. Each answer is counted when it arrives, and a run stops when the total passes the limit. Up to 150,000, default 40,000.

Tokens are the pieces of text the model reads and writes, about four characters each. They are what you pay for.

{related: harness-steps, harness-cost, harness-output}

### Maximum web searches {#harness-searches}

How many web searches the whole run may make, for agents that have web search switched on. Each search costs a small extra fee at the provider. 0 to 10, default 3.

Set 0 to forbid searching.

{related: harness-cost, harness-tokens}

### Maximum tokens of one reply {#harness-output}

The longest reply one agent may write, in tokens. A longer reply is cut and the replay shows a warning. 1,000 to 4,000, default 4,000.

A lower value keeps answers short and cheap. In Create, an agent can have a lower value of its own.

{related: harness-tokens}

### How long to wait for the model {#harness-timeout}

How long a request to the model may wait for an answer before it is given up. 30 to 120 seconds, default 60.

A busy model can be slow: raise it if requests time out, lower it if you do not want to wait. The total time of a run is fixed at 270 seconds.

{related: harness-retries}

### Tries after a failure {#harness-retries}

How many times a failed call is tried again. Only errors that can pass are retried: a busy provider (429, 500, 502, 503, 529) and network errors. A wrong key or a bad request never is. 0 to 3, default 1.

Every try counts as a step, and the replay warns each time.

{related: harness-retry-delay, harness-timeout, harness-steps}

### Wait before each try {#harness-retry-delay}

How long to wait before each new try, 1 to 10 seconds, default 1.5.

A longer wait gives a busy provider time to recover, but it uses up the total time of the run.

{related: harness-retries}

### Cost cap {#harness-cost}

Stops a run when its estimated cost passes this amount, from $0.10 to $5.00, or Off (the default).

The cost is added up after each answer from the prices in the catalog, so a run can go a little over before it stops. Only agents on a paid key count; agents on a free Gemini key cost nothing. The real bill at the provider can differ a little.

{related: harness-tokens, harness-steps}

### Repeated answers {#harness-repeat}

Stops a run when the same agent gives the same answer 2 to 4 times in a row, or Off. Answers count as the same when they match word for word, ignoring capital letters and spacing. Default 3.

A loop spends your money and gets nowhere, so this is on by default. It matters most for a supervisor and for debates.

{related: harness-steps, harness-handoff}

### Size of a handoff {#harness-handoff}

The most characters one agent passes on to the next, 2,000 to 20,000, or Off (the default).

A longer answer is cut, the next agent is told, and the replay shows a warning but keeps the whole answer. It keeps long chains cheap. About 4 characters make one token.

{related: harness-tokens, harness-repeat}

### Web search {#harness-web-search}

The master switch for web search in your runs, on Settings, Harness, Tools. When it is off, no agent searches, even one that has its own web search switch on, and a replay says so. When it is on, each agent still needs its own switch in Create, and the limit of searches and the cost cap still apply.

Searching costs a small extra fee at the provider and brings text from the web into the run. An agent that does not need the web should not have it.

{related: harness-web-content, create-tools, harness-searches}

### Web content is data {#harness-web-content}

Anything an agent reads from the web is untrusted: a page can contain words written to look like instructions, which is called prompt injection. Every agent that can search is told that text from the web is data, not instructions, and the replay marks search results as untrusted web content.

This is a fixed rule, it cannot be turned off, and it is no guarantee: a model can still be fooled. That is why an agent should have the web only when it needs it, and why you read what comes out. The articles Prompt injection, Trust boundaries and Least privilege explain it.

{related: harness-web-search, replay-web-content}

### Fallback model {#harness-fallback}

What happens when an agent still fails after all its tries, for example because the provider stays busy. Off: the run stops and you can use Retry yourself. Suggest (the default): the run stops and the Retry menu marks the model it suggests. Switch automatically: the agent gets one more try with another model of the same provider and the same key.

The fallback is always another model of the same provider: Fast is replaced by Balanced, Balanced by Fast and Strong by Balanced, so it is never costlier than Balanced. A free Gemini key is never switched to a model that needs a paid key. A different provider is never used without you. It applies only to errors that can pass, not to a wrong key or a bad request. The try counts as a step, the cost is worked out for the model that answered, and the cost cap still applies. The replay says which model took over.

{related: harness-retries, harness-cost, replay-warnings}

## Keyboard shortcuts

Press **?** anywhere for the full list. The main ones:

- **g** then a letter goes to a page (g p Play, g c Create, g r Result, g l Learn, g h Help). **g** then **b**, **f** or **i** open GitHub to report a bug, suggest a feature or see the open issues. The letters can be changed in Settings.
- **Space** plays or pauses, the arrows step or restart, **Tab** goes round the player and its tabs, **W A S D** move between the agents and **Enter** inspects one.
- **q** swaps between the library and the player, **b** shows or hides the library, **i** imports a run, **o** loads the last run and **/** searches.

## Bugs and ideas {#bugs}

Found a bug or have an idea? Tell us on GitHub. You need a free GitHub account.

- [Report a bug](https://github.com/cimenta/agent-atlas-academy/issues/new?template=bug_report.md)
- [Suggest a feature](https://github.com/cimenta/agent-atlas-academy/issues/new?template=feature_request.md)
- [See open issues](https://github.com/cimenta/agent-atlas-academy/issues)

The keyboard shortcuts **g** then **b**, **f** or **i** open the same pages in a new tab. Please do not post an API key or private text in an issue.

## Language

Use the language buttons at the top to switch between English and Czech. Your choice is remembered in your browser. Articles that are not translated yet are shown in English. In Learn, topics marked **is coming** describe something Agent Atlas does not do yet.

## Good to know

Watching a replay does not call any AI service, so it is free. Running your own experiment uses your own key (Anthropic or Google) and costs money at the provider; a Gemini key on Google's free tier costs nothing, and agents on it are counted as free. Agent Atlas remembers your language, settings and runs only in this browser, and your key only if you choose to, encrypted with a password that cannot be recovered.
